; The one variable: a ROUGHNESS MAP that arrives on the EXTERNAL `.tres`
; material path.
;
; Every other roughness-bearing fixture declares its StandardMaterial3D as a
; `[sub_resource]` of its own `.tscn`, and the only fixtures whose material is an
; external `.tres` carry scalars alone. So the two halves of the question — does a
; `.tres` material reach a primitive mesh at all, and does its `roughness_texture`
; survive the trip — are invisible to every other scene: a regression in either
; leaves the corpus green while this surface silently turns into the renderer's
; default material (mid-grey, ROUGHNESS 0.8) or into a flat `roughness = 1.0`.
;
; The map is a hard horizontal split, glossy (0.10) over rough (0.90), sampled
; raw as a non-colour map. It is legible as PIXELS rather than as a probe: the
; sphere's upper cap carries a small, hard specular highlight and its lower cap a
; broad dim one, with the seam a sharp ring across the middle. Drop the map and
; both caps become the same dull matte; bind it to a colour slot instead and the
; albedo goes banded.
;
; The dielectric is deliberate — `metallic = 0`, so the sky's reflection is the
; 0.04 Fresnel floor and the picture is the DIRECT light's specular lobe rather
; than a mirrored environment, which no roughness map could be arbitrated
; through. Authoring a DirectionalLight3D at all is what takes Godot's editor
; preview sun away (ADR-0025), leaving its preview environment; its
; `light_energy = 2.0` is the ordinary authored brightness that a dark enough
; albedo still resolves well short of clipping under, so the roughness signal is
; not thrown away in a saturated highlight.
[gd_scene load_steps=3 format=3]

[ext_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" path="res://unit-external-material-roughness.tres" id="1_mat"]

[sub_resource type="SphereMesh" id="Sphere_1"]
radius = 1.0
height = 2.0

[node name="ExternalMaterialRoughness" type="Node3D"]

; Basis rows put local -Z — the direction the light travels — down-and-forward at
; 40 degrees, which lands the specular highlight inside the GLOSSY band, well
; clear of the seam. The two caps are then read against each other rather than
; through a highlight that straddles both.
[node name="Sun" type="DirectionalLight3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 0.766044, 0.642788, 0, -0.642788, 0.766044, 0, 3, 3)
light_energy = 2.0

[node name="Ball" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
mesh = SubResource("Sphere_1")
surface_material_override/0 = ExtResource("1_mat")
